course: chinese-street
lesson: 8
title: Colours
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Picking crayons with Ming you hear a little «-sè» on the end of every colour: hóngsè, lánsè, huángsè. sè is the word for colour, just as yánsè is «colour» itself in «nǐ xǐhuan shénme yánsè?» (what colour do you like?).
explain: Colours carry feelings. Red is the happy, lucky colour of festivals; white is the colour of funerals, so white flowers are not a birthday present. Even writing someone's name in red ink is avoided, because it was once used for the names of the dead.
explain: Watch the sounds: lǜ (green) needs the new ü — say «ee» with lips rounded for «oo» — or it turns into lù (road). And never give a man a green hat: it is a famous joke about being made a fool of.
explain: Brown is «coffee colour», kāfēisè, and orange the colour is chéngsè, while the fruit has its own name.
fact: hóngsè («HONG?-suh») is red, the colour of luck, happiness and celebration; writing a name in red ink is unlucky.
fact: huángsè («HWAHNG?-suh») is yellow; huáng (yellow) sounds like huáng (emperor), and in old stories only the emperor wore the yellow robe.
fact: lánsè («LAHN?-suh») is blue; the blue of the sky can also be called qīng, a word for blue or green.
fact: lǜsè («lü-suh», lips rounded as for «oo» while saying «ee») is green; lǜ is not lù (road), and a green hat is a joke about being made a fool of.
fact: báisè («BYE?-suh») is white, the colour of mourning; white flowers and white envelopes are not birthday gifts.
fact: hēisè («HAY-suh») is black; hēi also means dark (tiān hēi le = it's got dark), and «hēi!» alone is a cheeky «hey!».
fact: zǐsè («DZ-suh», dipping) is purple; dipping zǐ is purple, falling zì is a character or letter.
fact: fěnsè («FUN-suh», dipping) is pink, also called fěnhóngsè (powder-red); teasing boys who like pink is old thinking, not a rule.
fact: chéngsè («CHUNG?-suh») is orange the colour; the fruit is chéngzi or júzi.
fact: huīsè («HWAY-suh») is grey; huī means ash or dust, and huīxīn means losing heart.
fact: kāfēisè («KAH-fay-suh», coffee colour) is brown; zōngsè is another word for brown.
fact: nǐ xǐhuan shénme yánsè? («nee SHEE-hwahn SHUN-muh YEN?-suh») means what colour do you like?; you answer wǒ xǐhuan lánsè (I like blue).
exercise: What is hóngsè? | Red | Yellow | Pink | Brown
exercise: What does red mean at Chinese festivals? | Luck, happiness and celebration | Danger, anger and a warning | Sadness and loss in the family | Stop and keep out
exercise: Why should you not write a friend's name in red ink? | It was once used for the names of the dead | Red ink is for teachers only, by school law | It means you secretly hate them | It wastes the lucky colour on paper
exercise: huángsè is | Yellow | Grey | Blue | Orange
exercise: In old stories, who wore the yellow robe? | Only the emperor | Every child at New Year | Doctors and nurses | Monks and nuns
exercise: What is lánsè? | Blue | Green | Purple | Black
exercise: What colour can qīng be? | Blue or green | Red or pink | Black or white | Grey only
exercise: How do you say green? | lǜsè, with the rounded ü | lùsè, like road | lánsè | hēisè
exercise: Why is a green hat a bad present for a man? | It is a famous joke about being made a fool of | Green is the colour worn at funerals | Giving any hat is rude in China | Green means poor
exercise: What colour is linked with funerals? | White | Red | Yellow | Pink
exercise: «tiān hēi le» means | It has got dark | The sky is blue | It is snowing | The sun is out
exercise: zǐsè is | Purple | Pink | Brown | Grey
exercise: fěnsè is | Pink | Purple | Orange | White
exercise: What is chéngsè? | Orange, the colour | An orange you can eat | Grey | Gold
exercise: huīsè is | Grey | Brown | Black | Blue
exercise: Why is brown called kāfēisè? | It means coffee colour | It is the colour of tea leaves | kāfēi means dark | It is the emperor's colour
exercise: How do you ask what colour someone likes? | nǐ xǐhuan shénme yánsè? | nǐ xǐhuan shénme míngzi? | nǐ jiào shénme yánsè? | duōshao qián?
exercise: What is the sè at the end of each colour? | The word for colour | A word meaning very | A plural ending | The word for paint
test: «get ming's dad a green hat for his birthday, he likes green» What is wrong? | A green hat is a joke about being made a fool of | Nothing, green hats bring a whole year of good luck | Hats mean goodbye | Green is unlucky on Mondays and birthdays
test: You bring white flowers to Ming's birthday. What might his granny think? | White is for funerals, not parties | White is the luckiest colour | White means «I love you» | White flowers are only for teachers
test: «I'm doing my name in red pen, looks sick» Why might Ming's mum stop you? | Names in red ink were once for the dead | Red pen is only for teachers to mark with | It is too bright and hurts the eyes | Red pen smudges
test: «lùsè, the road colour lol» What did you mean to say? | lǜsè, green, with the rounded ü | lánsè, blue, like the sky above | hēisè, black | huīsè, grey, like a rainy day
test: «ugh his little bro wants pink, boys can't like pink» What is right? | That's old thinking, not a rule | Yes, it is a Chinese law | Yes, pink is for grannies | No, pink is unlucky for everyone
test: You ask for a chéngsè to eat. Why does Ming laugh? | chéngsè is the colour; the fruit is chéngzi | chéngsè is a bitter green vegetable | chéngsè means a cup of coffee | chéngsè is a rude word in the playground
test: Ming jumps out shouting «hēi!». What does he mean? | A cheeky «hey!» | Black! | It's dark! | Goodbye!
test: What would happen if you said zì instead of zǐ for purple? | You'd say «character, letter» instead | It would mean pink, a lighter kind of purple | It would mean yellow | Nothing, they are the very same word
test: In a cartoon an emperor's robe is painted green. What would real old stories say? | It should be yellow, the emperor's colour | It should be white, the colour of good luck | It should be pink | Any colour was fine for an emperor
test: «my dog's kāfēisè» What colour is the dog? | Brown | Black | Grey | Orange
test: Ming's mum looks out: «tiān hēi le, huí jiā!» What does she want? | You to go home, it's got dark | You to wear black | You to get the black bag | You to look at the stars
exam: Identify the colour customarily associated with good fortune and celebration in Chinese culture. | Red | White | Green | Grey
exam: Ascertain the reason for which the presentation of a green hat to a man is to be avoided. | It is a well-known jest concerning a man made a fool of | Green is the colour customarily worn in mourning | Hats may not be given as gifts under any circumstances | Green signifies poverty
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the colour white in Chinese tradition. | It is associated with funerals and mourning | It is reserved for wedding celebrations alone | It signifies wealth and prosperity | It was worn exclusively by the emperors
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the element sè in the names of colours? | It is the word signifying colour itself | It is an intensifying particle | It indicates the plural | It denotes a particular shade of red or orange
